WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:06.705
Our abduction took place so quickly that I shivered all over. Who were our captors?

2
00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:11.967
They were undoubtedly some new sort of pirates, who explored the sea in their own way.

3
00:00:11.968 --> 00:00:17.110
The narrow hatch closed above us and we were surrounded by complete darkness.

4
00:00:17.111 --> 00:00:23.197
My eyes, dazzled by the brightness outside the vessel, couldn't adjust to the lack of light inside.

5
00:00:23.198 --> 00:00:28.435
The men forced us down a metal ladder. At the bottom of the ladder, a door opened.

6
00:00:28.436 --> 00:00:33.268
We were shoved into a room and the door immediately shut behind us with a bang.

7
00:00:33.269 --> 00:00:38.358
The three of us were alone, and I couldn't even begin to imagine where we were.

8
00:00:38.359 --> 00:00:42.858
My eyes were still not able to discern even the faintest glimmer of light.

9
00:00:42.859 --> 00:00:50.263
"These people are the worst hosts I've ever met!" shouted Ned. "Fortunately I still have my knife with me."

10
00:00:50.264 --> 00:00:53.755
"Calm yourself, my friend," said Conseil. "Yes,

11
00:00:53.756 --> 00:00:59.668
Ned," I said. "Don't make the situation worse with senseless violence. Perhaps

12
00:00:59.669 --> 00:01:06.072
we can get our captors to listen to us, but first let's try to figure out where we are." I

13
00:01:06.073 --> 00:01:13.986
began to grope my way around the room. In five steps I came to a wall made of iron plates bolted together. Turning

14
00:01:13.987 --> 00:01:19.897
around, I ran into a wooden table around which were arranged several stools. The

15
00:01:19.898 --> 00:01:25.905
floor of this prison was concealed under thick mats that deadened the sound of footsteps. The

16
00:01:25.906 --> 00:01:33.722
bare walls revealed no trace of window or door. I guessed that the cabin measured about twenty feet by ten. Half

17
00:01:33.723 --> 00:01:39.965
an hour had passed without any change in our situation when suddenly a bright light went on. I

18
00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:43.549
squeezed my eyes tight against the light. When

19
00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:50.040
I slowly opened them, I saw that the source of this brightness was an electric light in the ceiling. The

20
00:01:50.041 --> 00:01:57.373
sudden illumination of the cabin allowed me to examine it more carefully, but it only contained the table and five stools. We

21
00:01:57.374 --> 00:02:02.445
couldn't hear a sound; everything seemed dead onboard this mysterious vessel. Was

22
00:02:02.446 --> 00:02:09.865
it floating on the surface of the sea or had it dived into the ocean's depths? I had no idea. But

23
00:02:09.866 --> 00:02:17.516
then we did hear a sound: the noise of bolts sliding. A door opened in a wall and two men appeared. One

24
00:02:17.517 --> 00:02:22.857
was short and very muscular with thick black hair and a mustache. The

25
00:02:22.858 --> 00:02:28.682
second man was tall with a large forehead, straight nose, and fine teeth. One

26
00:02:28.683 --> 00:02:34.079
peculiar feature was that his cold, black eyes were set rather far apart. After

27
00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:42.087
observing him for a few moments, I decided that he was a calm, self-confident man full of energy and courage. But

28
00:02:42.088 --> 00:02:47.579
whether this man was thirty-five or fifty years of age, I could not say. The two

29
00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:54.484
strangers wore fur caps and boots, and their clothes were made from a fabric that I couldn't identify. The taller

30
00:02:54.485 --> 00:03:00.484
man, who was evidently the captain of the vessel, carefully examined us in silence. Then he

31
00:03:00.485 --> 00:03:05.643
said something to his lieutenant in a language that I couldn't understand. The second

32
00:03:05.644 --> 00:03:10.635
man replied to him and stared at me as if waiting for me to speak. I said

33
00:03:10.636 --> 00:03:15.563
in French that I didn't know his language, but he didn't seem to understand me. "Master, if

34
00:03:15.564 --> 00:03:23.214
you were to tell our story, perhaps these gentlemen might understand some words," suggested Conseil. I began

35
00:03:23.215 --> 00:03:27.047
to describe our adventures onboard the Abraham Lincoln . Then

36
00:03:27.048 --> 00:03:36.277
I introduced us as Professor Pierre Aronnax, his servant Conseil, and Mr. Ned Land, the harpooner. The

37
00:03:36.278 --> 00:03:42.539
men listened to us quietly, but there was nothing in their faces to indicate that they'd understood a word. "Perhaps

38
00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:49.190
they understand English," I said to Ned. "You speak it better than I do. Give it a try." Once

39
00:03:49.191 --> 00:03:58.015
more Ned told our story in English, but with the same result. Next Conseil tried in German with no success. I

40
00:03:58.016 --> 00:04:06.102
even attempted to tell it in Latin. The two strangers exchanged some words in their unknown language and left the room. Ned

41
00:04:06.103 --> 00:04:14.261
was furious. "We spoke to those rogues in French, English, German, and Latin, and they didn't have the politeness to answer!" "Calm

42
00:04:14.262 --> 00:04:18.769
yourself, Ned," I said. "Anger won't do us any good." "But

43
00:04:18.770 --> 00:04:24.166
don't you see, Professor? We'll die of hunger in this iron prison!" he replied. "We

44
00:04:24.167 --> 00:04:30.245
won't starve," said Conseil calmly. "We can hold out for some time yet." "My

45
00:04:30.246 --> 00:04:39.507
friends, we must not despair," I said. "Meanwhile I can't decide if our captors are from Spain, Turkey, Arabia, or India. Their language

46
00:04:39.508 --> 00:04:42.071
is quite incomprehensible." As I

47
00:04:42.072 --> 00:04:47.650
said these words, the door opened and a steward entered with fresh clothes for us. While we

48
00:04:47.651 --> 00:04:51.071
hurried to dress, the steward set the table. "Now that's

49
00:04:51.072 --> 00:04:53.896
more like it!" said Conseil. "Bah!" said

50
00:04:53.897 --> 00:04:58.896
Ned. "What do you suppose they eat here—tortoise liver?" The place

51
00:04:58.897 --> 00:05:02.388
settings were elegant and in perfect taste. We were

52
00:05:02.389 --> 00:05:10.880
undoubtedly dealing with civilized people, and if it weren't for the electric light, I could imagine we were dining in one of the finest hotels in Europe. All the

53
00:05:10.881 --> 00:05:18.118
dishes and silverware were inscribed with the letter N and a Latin motto: Mobilis in mobili . "What

54
00:05:18.119 --> 00:05:23.372
does that mean, Professor?" asked Ned, running a finger over the letters. "

55
00:05:23.373 --> 00:05:27.084
N is undoubtedly the captain's initial," I said.

56
00:05:27.085 --> 00:05:34.364
"And the inscription means mobile within the mobile element or in this case moving through water

57
00:05:34.365 --> 00:05:38.610
." We didn't talk much after that, being too busy devouring our

58
00:05:38.611 --> 00:05:45.428
food. I recognized some fish dishes, but I wasn't sure if some of the other things we ate were animals or

59
00:05:45.429 --> 00:05:48.928
vegetables. At least our hosts were not going to let us

60
00:05:48.929 --> 00:05:54.436
starve. After eating, my two companions settled themselves on the floor of the cabin and were soon

61
00:05:54.437 --> 00:05:58.753
asleep. But I lay awake wondering where we were and with

62
00:05:58.754 --> 00:06:02.920
whom. At one point I thought the vessel was sinking to the bottom of the

63
00:06:02.921 --> 00:06:08.253
sea. Tormented by nightmares, I tossed and turned before finally falling into a deep

64
00:06:08.254 --> 00:06:11.754
sleep. When I awoke I didn't know how long we'd been

65
00:06:11.755 --> 00:06:16.737
sleeping. We must have used up most of the air in the cabin because it was difficult to

66
00:06:16.738 --> 00:06:23.570
breathe. Just as I was speculating about how the captain refreshed the air for the whole vessel, I smelled a sea breeze.

67
00:06:23.571 --> 00:06:29.143
The ship rolled, and I realized that it had risen to the surface to breathe like a whale.

68
00:06:29.144 --> 00:06:33.880
Ned and Conseil awoke and breathed in the pure, fresh air.

69
00:06:33.881 --> 00:06:36.555
"What time is it?" asked Ned.

70
00:06:36.556 --> 00:06:42.388
"I have no idea," I replied. "It's possible that we've slept for 24 hours."

71
00:06:42.389 --> 00:06:48.967
More time passed and Ned grew angry. There was no sound on the boat, and it didn't move.

72
00:06:48.968 --> 00:06:54.626
I felt terrified; Conseil remained calm; Ned roared with anger and hunger.

73
00:06:54.627 --> 00:07:00.959
Suddenly the door opened and the steward appeared. With a shout Ned threw the man to the floor.

74
00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:09.364
Conseil tried to pull Ned off the steward. I was just about to jump into the fray when I was stopped by hearing these words in French:

75
00:07:09.365 --> 00:07:17.413
"Be quiet, Mr. Land, and you, Professor Aronnax, will you kindly listen to me?"

